@@s001 Length and distance
@@s002 dato:x component,due:y component,ok:length
@@s003 The vector <b>v = [3, 4]</b>: an arrow that starts from the origin and reaches the point (3, 4). How long is it?
@@s004 Move <b>3</b> horizontally: the first component.
@@s005 And <b>4</b> vertically: the second component. The two components and the arrow form a right triangle, and the arrow is the hypotenuse.
@@s006 Pythagoras' theorem: length² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25, so length = √25 = <b>5</b>. It is written <b>‖v‖ = 5</b>, with double bars.
@@s007 dato:the components,ora:the squares,ok:the norm
@@s008 dato:the two points,ora:the difference,ok:the distance
@@s009 due:len counts the cells,ok:norm measures the arrow
@@s010 ok:L2 (Euclidean),due:L1 (Manhattan),ora:L∞ (maximum)
@@s011 dato:original vector,ok:unit vector
@@s012 Length and distance
@@s013 When a datum is a vector, the first question is "how big is it?" and the second "how far is it from another?". The answers are the <b>norm</b> and the <b>distance</b>. They serve to compare poses, measure errors and, later, for every loss.
@@s014 The norm: how long a vector is
@@s015 The <b>norm</b> of a vector v is written <span class="m">‖v‖</span>, with double vertical bars, and says how long its arrow is. It does not say in which direction it points: only how big it is.
@@s016 In 2D it is obtained with Pythagoras' theorem. The two components are the legs of a right triangle, the arrow is the hypotenuse: <span class="m">‖v‖ = √(v₁² + v₂²)</span>. For v = [3, 4]: √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5.
@@s017 In NumPy one writes <code>np.linalg.norm(v)</code>, or by hand <code>np.sqrt(np.sum(v**2))</code>: squares, sum, root.
@@s018 You asked: what is ‖v‖ for, with v = [3, 4]?
@@s019 ‖v‖ = 5 says <b>how big</b> v is, regardless of direction. In ML it serves for: the <b>distance</b> between two data (‖a − b‖, how similar two poses are), the <b>size of an error</b> (‖predicted − true‖), and for <b>normalising</b> (v / ‖v‖ = [0.6, 0.8] has length 1 and the same direction as v).
@@s020 v = [3, 4]: Pythagoras
@@s021 The norm in n dimensions
@@s022 The formula does not change with the number of components: one term is added for each.
@@s023 It is called the <b>L2</b> or <b>Euclidean</b> norm. The order of operations is the same as in the loss: first <b>each</b> square, then the sum, then the root. The root cannot be "distributed": √(9 + 16) = 5, while √9 + √16 = 7.
@@s024 A connection that comes back often: the sum of the squares of the components is the inner product of the vector with itself, so <span class="m">‖v‖ = √(v · v)</span> (<a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a>).
@@s025 ‖[1, 2, 2]‖, without drawing ℝ³
@@s026 Distance between two points
@@s027 The <b>Euclidean distance</b> between a and b is the norm of their difference:
@@s028 Definition
@@s029 <span class="m">d(a, b) = ‖a − b‖ = √( Σᵢ (aᵢ − bᵢ)² )</span>. It is 0 only if a = b, and it is symmetric: d(a, b) = d(b, a).
@@s030 Why the difference: a − b is the vector that goes from b to a, and its length is exactly how far apart they are. With two data described by vectors of features, the distance is the simplest way to measure how much they <b>resemble</b> each other: small = similar. It is the goal "calculate the Euclidean distance in n-dimensional spaces and explain its relation to the inner product" of week 1.
@@s031 Distance between two poses (vectors of 50 components)
@@s032 18 poses, each a vector of 50 numbers (25 points × 2 coordinates).
@@s033 The difference of two NumPy vectors is <b>element by element</b>: a new vector of 50 numbers.
@@s034 Its norm: how far apart the two poses are (3.949 between poses 0 and 1). Small = similar poses.
@@s035 The same result written with the formula: squares, sum, root.
@@s036 d(p, q) = ‖q − p‖
@@s037 len versus norm: two "lengths" in Python
@@s038 In Italian we say "lunghezza" for two different things, and in Python they are two different functions.
@@s039 <code>len(v)</code> returns the <b>number of components</b>: for [3, 4] it is 2. It is the dimension of the vector. It serves to check that two vectors have the same dimension before adding, subtracting or taking their inner product.
@@s040 <code>np.linalg.norm(v)</code> returns the <b>geometric length</b>: for [3, 4] it is 5. To know which of two vectors is "longer" in the geometric sense one compares the norms, not the <code>len</code>.
@@s041 You asked: compare the length of two arrays in Python
@@s042 For the dimension: <code>len(a) == len(b)</code>. For the size: <code>np.linalg.norm(a) &gt; np.linalg.norm(b)</code>.
@@s043 Two lengths for the same array
@@s044 Other norms: L1 and L∞
@@s045 The Euclidean norm is not the only one. All of them measure "how big" a vector is, but they weigh the components in different ways.
@@s046 Norm
@@s047 Formula
@@s048 For v = [3, 4]
@@s049 NumPy
@@s050 <b>L2</b> (Euclidean)
@@s051 <code>np.linalg.norm(v)</code>
@@s052 <b>L1</b> (Manhattan)
@@s053 <code>np.linalg.norm(v, 1)</code>
@@s054 <b>L∞</b> (maximum)
@@s055 <span class="m">maxᵢ |vᵢ|</span>
@@s056 <code>np.linalg.norm(v, np.inf)</code>
@@s057 The single bars |vᵢ| denote the <b>absolute value</b>: the number without sign. L1 is the "city block" route (3 to the right + 4 up, without cutting diagonally); L∞ looks only at the largest component. Different norms give different ideas of "near": the choice matters in evaluating models (week 11, where the mean of the errors in L1 is called MAE) and in regularisation.
@@s058 Values computed with NumPy.
@@s059 The points at distance 1: circle, diamond, square
@@s060 Unit vector and normalisation
@@s061 A vector of norm 1 is called <b>unit</b> (<i>unit vector</i>). Every non-zero vector is <b>normalised</b> by dividing it by its norm: <span class="m">v̂ = v / ‖v‖</span>. The hat on v indicates the unit version.
@@s062 Dividing every component by the same positive number does not change the direction, it only changes the length, which becomes 1. For [3, 4]: [3/5, 4/5] = [0.6, 0.8], and indeed 0.36 + 0.64 = 1.
@@s063 It is needed when only the <b>direction</b> matters: for example to measure the angle between two vectors, or to compare two data without their scale mattering. Be careful not to confuse it with the "normalisation" of data of week 7, which rescales the <b>features</b> (the columns) and not the individual vectors.
@@s064 v / ‖v‖ lands on the unit circle
